[{:db/id -1
  :receipt/id "2026-08-18-one-worker-five-configs"
  :receipt/date "2026-08-18"
  :source/dataset "receipt"
  :receipt/title "Five configs publish one Worker, and the broken build was the only thing stopping one of them"
  :receipt/body
  "I set out to repair the 13 builds `verify-shadow-source-paths` found. Before
   repairing them I checked which were alive, and one of them should NOT be
   repaired yet.

   ## The finding

   `kotobase-protocols-worker` is declared by FIVE wrangler configs with THREE
   different route sets over seven hostnames:

     [6] gftdcojp/net-kotobase-git/protocols-worker
     [0] kotoba-lang/kotobase-protocols-worker
     [6] net-kotobase/control-plane/protocols-worker      <- the live one
     [7] network-awai/net-kotobase-enterprise-wave2/protocols-worker
     [6] network-awai/net-kotobase/protocols-worker

   The live deployment (version 120f3e08, published today) comes from
   control-plane and serves six hosts: s3, atproto, pinning, sparql, gremlin,
   graphql. The enterprise-wave2 copy declares those six **plus
   cypher.kotobase.net** — which is served today by a different Worker,
   `net-kotobase-cypher` (version d00e45b5, also published today).

   A Worker is addressed by NAME at publish time. There is no fast-forward
   check. So which hostnames `kotobase-protocols-worker` ends up serving is
   decided by whichever config went last.

   ## Why this changes what to do about the drift

   The enterprise-wave2 copy **cannot compile** — it is one of the 13, missing
   the same three source-paths I fixed by hand in two other repos this morning.
   Its build stops before it can publish anything.

   **That broken build is currently the only thing preventing a topology
   change.** Repairing its source-paths — which is exactly what
   `verify-shadow-source-paths` prints as the remedy — would arm it.

   One detector's remedy is the other detector's hazard. Neither is wrong; they
   have to be read together. I have recorded that in both entries rather than
   silently declining to fix one of the 13.

   ## The general shape

   Scanning every wrangler config under orgs/ (excluding agent worktrees and
   pre-adoption backups, which carry real config files and manufacture
   collisions nobody can act on):

     892 configs, 890 named
     263 names declared by more than one config
       253 with IDENTICAL route sets   — duplicate checkouts. Publishing a
                                         stale one ships older code to the same
                                         hostnames.
        10 with DIFFERENT route sets   — the hostname set itself depends on
                                         which config went last.

   The 253 are one aggregated warn, not 253 findings. Burying ten actionable
   items under 253 is how a detector stops being read — the same mistake the
   first version of the source-path detector made when it reported 41 of 41
   repos broken.

   The 10, worst first by hostnames at stake: net-kotobase-ipfs (2 configs, 7
   hosts, one declaring 7 routes and the other 1), kotobase-protocols-worker (5,
   7), net-kotobase (4, 5), kotobase-cf-wasm-staging (4, 3), magatama-sh1n5h1x
   (4, 2), magatama-mng4k4x1, etzhayyim-authz, kotobase-authn,
   itonami-fleet-dispatch, kotobase-mail.

   ## Precedent

   ADR-2607250000 records kotobase.net's signup funnel returning to 404 eleven
   minutes after being fixed, because a second session published from an older
   checkout. That was the SAME-routes case: older code, same hostnames. These
   ten are the harder case, because the hostname assignment changes too.

   ## Not done

   Nothing was consolidated. Which config owns a Worker name is a naming and
   ownership decision, not a build fix, and picking one would silently retire
   the others. The detector reports; the decision is the owner's."
  :receipt/measured
